  "problem": "Which of the following statements are logically equivalent?\n\n- $\u00ac(\u00acX\u2227\u00acY)$\n- $\u00acX\u2228\u00acY$\n- $\u00ac(X\u2227Y)$",
  "worked_solution": "Two statements are logically equivalent if they have the same truth table. So, we construct a truth table for the three given statements:\n\n| $\\,X\\,$ | $\\,Y\\,$ | $\\,\u00ac(\u00acX\u2227\u00acY)\\,$ | $\\,\u00acX\u2228\u00acY\\,$ | $\\,\u00ac(X\u2227Y)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n\nWe see that only the statements $\u00acX\u2228\u00acY$ and $\u00ac(X\u2227Y)$ have the same truth table, so they are logically equivalent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
